      "final_message": "I rewrote `merge_intervals` in `solution.py`. Both tests in `python3 -m unittest -v test_public.py` pass.\n\nThe function now does what the README describes:\n- **Checks the input:** a `TypeError` if `intervals` isn't a list, and a `ValueError` for any interval that isn't a two-item list or tuple, uses non-int or bool values, or has start greater than end.\n- **Leaves the input alone:** it copies the pairs before sorting and never changes the original list or its items.\n- **Merges:** it sorts by start and merges an interval into the previous one when it begins at or before that one's end, so `[1,2],[2,3]` becomes `[1,3]`. It returns a new list of `[start, end]` pairs, and an empty list for empty input.\n\nI also ran a few extra cases directly, beyond the two public tests: bools, wrong lengths, floats, a tuple instead of a list, touching intervals, and checking the input is unchanged. All behaved as documented.\n\nDONE: Repaired merge_intervals to validate input, avoid mutation, and merge overlapping/touching intervals sorted by start; public tests pass.",
